Commit:     e72da2b53805894878023d01949a25a082e0a5cb (2026-09-26)
Paths:      codex-rs/rollout/src/recorder.rs
License:    Apache-2.0. Verified at the source 2026-09-26 (LICENSE and NOTICE
            already present in this directory from the block above).
Date verified: 2026-09-26

**Note on scope.** ROADMAP Phase 3 (0055) names `rollout/` (a JSONL session
log) as the resumable-transcript source. At this commit `rollout/` is 2,000+
lines coupling the JSONL log to SQLite-backed thread listing, cross-thread
search, archived-thread migration and a legacy ghost-snapshot rewrite -- none
of which kGraf has an equivalent for (no thread store, no multi-agent
forking model), so the crate is not vendored whole. What transfers is its
core write/resume shape.

Ported (Rust to Rust) into crates/kgraf-session/src/transcript.rs
(`JsonlTranscript`): `JsonlWriter::write_line` (serialize one record, append a
newline, `write_all`, then `flush`, so a crash cannot lose an acknowledged
append) and `open_rollout_for_append` / `ensure_rollout_is_newline_terminated`
(open in append mode; a file not ending in `\n` means the last write was cut
short).

**Deliberately different from upstream, and why (SR-008).**
`ensure_rollout_is_newline_terminated` pads a torn last line with a trailing
newline and continues; `load_rollout_items` then finds that padded line fails
to parse as JSON, logs a `warn!`, increments a `parse_errors` counter, and
*keeps going* -- the torn record is silently absent from the returned items.
That is exactly the failure class SR-008 exists to name: a torn last line
reads as "nothing to see here" instead of as a fact the resuming code must
act on. kGraf's `JsonlTranscript::open` refuses to open a file with a
torn or corrupt tail (returns `SessionError::TornTranscriptLine`); a
`JsonlTranscript::open_repairing_torn_tail` is provided for a caller that
wants to resume anyway, and it returns the discarded bytes as a named
`TornTail` value rather than dropping them into a log line, so the fact is in
the type the caller has to handle, not just in text.

