Ported (subset, reworked) into
crates/kgraf-sandbox/src/sandbox/linux/seccomp.rs: the unconditional
denials of ptrace, process_vm_readv/writev and io_uring_setup/enter/register;
socket-domain conditions on socket and socketpair; EPERM as the match action
with allow as the default; compiling the program with `seccompiler` (0.5.0,
Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause, the crate upstream depends on; license verified
from its crates.io metadata and LICENSE-APACHE / LICENSE-BSD-3-Clause files).

Deliberately different from upstream:
  - socket(AF_UNIX) is denied in every mode; upstream's restricted mode allows
    it and denies connect/sendto, which leaves sendmsg to a path open.
  - With network allowed, socket() is limited to AF_INET/AF_INET6; upstream
    installs no filter then, so every unix socket stays reachable.
  - Also denied: keyctl/add_key/request_key, the mount family, setns, bpf,
    perf_event_open, pidfd_getfd, ioctl TIOCSTI/TIOCLINUX.
  - x86_64: x32-ABI syscall numbers are denied (seccompiler checks only the
    audit arch, which x32 shares).
  - No new namespaces: unshare and clone with any CLONE_NEW* bit get EPERM,
    clone3 gets ENOSYS (libc falls back to clone). Also denied: userfaultfd,
    open_by_handle_at, name_to_handle_at, kcmp, process_madvise.
  - The filter is compiled in the parent and installed in the forked child
    with one raw seccomp(2) call (no allocation after fork).

Not taken: bubblewrap (bwrap.rs, bundled_bwrap.rs, launcher.rs,
linux_run_main.rs). Upstream gets namespaces and read-only mounts from a
bwrap binary. kGraf enters user, mount, IPC, PID (and, on network deny,
network) namespaces with direct syscalls in
crates/kgraf-sandbox/src/sandbox/linux/namespaces.rs, its own code, so no
helper binary is shipped or looked up on PATH. Also not taken: the proxy
routing and managed-network modes (proxy_routing.rs, proxy_lifecycle.rs),
WSL and daemon-socket mounts.

Followed in design, not copied (2026-10-08), for the Windows sandbox user
(decision 0097; crates/kgraf-sandbox/src/sandbox_user/): read at commit
9b738582b13c2cdbeff54af0afd04c50c3e7ba09, codex-rs/windows-sandbox-rs (Apache-2.0).
Taken as design: an offline and an online sandbox user; a password reset on every
setup; hiding the users from the sign-in screen; firewall block rules scoped by
LocalUserAuthorizedList to the offline user's SID, with its non-loopback address
literals and loopback blocks that cut out the proxy ports; the check that local
firewall rules take effect; a runner that starts as the sandbox user.
Deliberately different: passwords come from the system random source (upstream uses
a non-cryptographic generator); the elevated step does not seal them, the user's own
unelevated process does (DPAPI for the current user; upstream uses machine-scope
DPAPI); the setup pipe is created by the user's own process, and each end checks the
other's process ID; the user's token carries no restricting SIDs (upstream keeps
them, and Schannel TLS fails there, openai/codex#17459).

Units 2 and 3 of the same design (2026-10-08), the runner and the launch path.
Taken as design: the runner is started as the sandbox user with
CreateProcessWithLogonW, which a standard user may call, and the command travels
over a pipe, so the call's 1,024-character command line limit does not apply; each
end of that pipe checks the other's process ID.
Deliberately different: the standard handles are given to the runner by the call and
passed on to the command, where upstream frames the three streams over its pipe; the
command's token makes the caller's logon SID deny-only, where upstream keeps that SID
and adds restricting SIDs; the command runs on a window station and desktop of its
own, admitting only the sandbox user and SYSTEM, where upstream grants its desktop to
the caller's logon SID; the runner writes protected access lists on its own process,
thread and token, and verifies them, before it starts the command; the launch
handshake has one deadline and ends the job when it passes.
Followed in design, not copied: the alternate window station for an untrusted
process, as Chromium's sandbox uses one (chromium sandbox/win/src/window.cc,
BSD-3-Clause). No code was taken, and nothing was read from that file.
